    "created_at": "2017-10-12T23:28:36Z",
    "updated_at": "2017-10-12T23:28:36Z",
    "body": "This is interesting. I wrote some web platform tests here and browsers seem to take contenteditable-ness as a very strong signal, which even overrides `draggable=\"true\"`. You cannot drag such links, in contradiction to https://html.spec.whatwg.org/#the-draggable-attribute .\r\n\r\nWhat's worse, browsers let `el.draggable` be true even if the element is not draggable due to contenteditable.\r\n\r\nI see three paths:\r\n\r\n- Codify existing browser behavior, by saying something like \"even in the auto or true state, an element that is contenteditable is not draggable\". This has the downside that you cannot rely on `el.draggable` to be a reliable indicator of whether an element is draggable.\r\n- Say that contenteditable elements are always in the false state. Make setting `el.draggable = true`, or setting the `draggable=\"true\"` content attribute, leave the element in the false state if it's contenteditable (although it will revert to its normal state if the contenteditable attribute is removed). This has the upside that now `el.draggable` is accurate, but the downside of requiring implementations to change, and that it's possible to have a non-draggable element with `draggable=\"true\"` in the markup.\r\n- Change implementations so that `draggable=\"true\"` (or `el.draggable = true`) overrides contenteditability and lets the element be dragged anyway, somehow. (Maybe if you very carefully drag from the border of the element.) I kind of doubt anyone wants to do this.\r\n\r\nAny thoughts?",
